SOAPBOX: CQ WW SSB report from *** The Center of the World *** E2A @ HS0ZGD
SOAPBOX: Conclusion: As a famous US Ham sometimes say We are doing great!
SOAPBOX: Meaning in this case, we actually did much better than we had anticipated,
SOAPBOX: before the start.
SOAPBOX: Well, things looked a bit bleak in the run up this contest, as our handyman,
SOAPBOX: climber, welder, antenna worker etc. etc. THON became a SK just a month prior
SOAPBOX: to the contest, less than 50 years old (stroke).
SOAPBOX: He's been part of the team for some 20 plus years, and has been a BIG asset.
SOAPBOX: The other huge challenge was too few operators. Some cancellations before the
SOAPBOX: contest made it hard to do Multi - Multi.
SOAPBOX: Although after some deliberations during Friday night, we decided to try for the
SOAPBOX: Multi - Multi category anyway.
SOAPBOX: Our main challenge now, is to find someone that can climb and help us with
SOAPBOX: maintenance.
SOAPBOX: Not easy at all, and unsuccessfully so far.
SOAPBOX: We have some high towers, up to 60 m. tall freestanding, and the monsoon storms
SOAPBOX: had (as usual) made its impact, as the two largest rotators were QRT, and the
SOAPBOX: C31XR antenna on another tower was QRT as well.
SOAPBOX: We only managed to repair parts of all the problems, but - once again it proved
SOAPBOX: helpful to have backup towers & antennas.
SOAPBOX: For the first time in our multi operator history, all transceivers and
SOAPBOX: amplifiers survived without flaws.
SOAPBOX: Murphy must have been too busy elsewhere this time around.
SOAPBOX: Our noise level though, is getting bigger and bigger every year, now at a level
SOAPBOX: that makes receiving very very challenging, as soon as all the Big Guns have
SOAPBOX: been worked.
SOAPBOX: So we can only apologize to all the stations that call us, that we are not able
SOAPBOX: to pull through, due to the "extreme" man made noise.
SOAPBOX: Over here in Southeast Asia, there is a huge amount of non compliant electrical
SOAPBOX: appliances around us, producing this QRN, making it very time consuming,
SOAPBOX: difficult end very expensive to battle this phenomena. It is only possible to
SOAPBOX: some extent.
SOAPBOX: Well, it was a thrill for me being called from someone in Trondheim, Norway,
SOAPBOX: even my LA local radio club LN2T, which we worked on 3 bands.
SOAPBOX: Many other highly satisfactory QSOs over the weekend happened. Many multipliers
SOAPBOX: in the Caribbean worked, although missing a few, were we simple could not break
SOAPBOX: the US&EU pileups.
SOAPBOX: It is a particularly tough task to accomplish here in Thailand, being so very
SOAPBOX: far away - 16400 km or 10200 miles, on the shortest path.
SOAPBOX: Working US stations is tough as well, with not that many in the log. 296 US out
SOAPBOX: of totally 5450 QSOs.
SOAPBOX: To give you an idea, my QTH (HS0ZGD) is typically some 14000 km or 8700 miles
SOAPBOX: away. Whereas London, UK, to for example Kansas, is about the half distance,
SOAPBOX: some 7000 kilometers.
SOAPBOX: The distances involved mean that we can only hear the average 100-watt station
SOAPBOX: with a dipole or vertical antenna when propagation is exceptionally good. Yet
SOAPBOX: these stations can hear us almost whenever propagation is present at all!
SOAPBOX: Understandably, many get frustrated when they call us and receive no answer.
SOAPBOX: Some comments on the DX clusters have even described us as “deaf”! Hi.
SOAPBOX: The thing that make it worth-while to continue to operate from over here,
SOAPBOX: although the age is getting to be a challenge, is the arrival of superb
SOAPBOX: operators and personalities year after year.
SOAPBOX: DL3DXX - Dietmar was here. He is already a veteran from this callsign & QTH.
SOAPBOX: And as the expert behind our huge MicroHam switching system, we would be in huge
SOAPBOX: problems without him.
SOAPBOX: JH4RHF -Jun and SP3LPG -Marek also were here, besides the "local
SOAPBOX: staff", including E25KAE - Bun, E21EIC - Champ, E20NKB - JC, HS0ZDY - Sam,
SOAPBOX: HS4RAY - Ray with his own support staff, and yours truly LA7JO - HS0ZGD.
SOAPBOX: We cannot compete on "the world stage" from here, the laws of physics
SOAPBOX: prevent that, but - we can have fun and compete on the Southeast Asia stage.
SOAPBOX: Our claimed score should be a new HS, zone 26 and ASEAN (Association of
SOAPBOX: Southeast Asian Nations) score in the category Multi operator Multi transmitter
SOAPBOX: PHONE. Our claimed score is about 8 million points, some 5450 QSOs and 650
SOAPBOX: multipliers.
SOAPBOX: So, in the morning at 07:00 local time, when the contest ended, we felt very
SOAPBOX: tired (of course) but, satisfied and happy.
SOAPBOX: CU in the WW CW part
SOAPBOX: 73 de E2A @HS0ZGD Stig
SOAPBOX: Phimai, Thailand
SOAPBOX: Siam DX Group
